kb-e1042          = [0.6, 0.8]   # help-center article, embeddings API
pdf-refund-policy = [3.0, 1.0]   # PDF page: sum of its paragraph vectors

def score(q, v):
    return sum(x * y for x, y in zip(q, v))

Statement: "score() ranks pdf-refund-policy above kb-e1042, although kb-e1042 points in exactly the same direction as the query."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. score() is the dot product: 0.6*0.6 + 0.8*0.8 = 1.0 for kb-e1042, but 0.6*3.0 + 0.8*1.0 = 2.6 for the PDF page. The dot product is |q| * |v| * cos(angle), so the page wins only because its vector is long (|v| = 3.16). Its cosine is 2.6 / 3.16 = 0.82, below kb-e1042''s 1.0. The misconception: a higher dot product means a more similar text. That holds only when every vector has the same length.', 1, 5
